    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "Ant colonies operate without central control. Individual ants respond to local interactions with other ants, and in the aggregate, this allows colonies to regulate their behavior and respond to changing conditions. Dr. Gordon’s lab investigates how red harvester ants (Pogonomyrmex barbatus) use the rate of interactions in task decisions. Previous work shows that the rate of incoming, successful foragers determines the rate of outgoing foragers (Greene et al. 2013) and that ants experience an increase in interactions shortly before they choose to forage (Pinter-Wollman et al. 2013). However, it had not been shown that ants that choose to forage experience a different rate of interactions from ants that choose to stay in the nest. By directly observing and recording interactions, I demonstrated that ants that chose to forage experienced a significantly higher rate of interactions than ants that did not forage. Additionally, these foraging ants spent significantly less time interacting with other ants than ants that ultimately chose not to forage. These conclusions were based on observing and recording all interactions experienced by 90 outgoing foragers and 44 non-foragers from three nests across three days. For each colony and day, I also quantified what percentage of ants that left the deeper nest ultimately engaged in foraging, nest maintenance, or neither, and I found similar outcome distributions among the colonies. Finally, I attempted to measure the decay rate of the effect of an interaction for foraging harvester ants of different colonies. Restraint in foraging is a heritable trait that contributes to reproductive success, so I hypothesized that I would find consistent differences in the decay rate of the experience of an interaction in ants of different colonies and that these differences may suggest a mechanism for differences in foraging regulation (Gordon 2013).",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
